#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "9b2415da-21b8-5a59-9b3f-2f30b624c36f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7194" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/1045aaad8025b20309b92477b4a1cf98e138064210c6fbc960c64ec26c9951df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/40cbaaff270daddf4b60115cc272fbc8b165b83b8823d19de2c19802617ebdb4.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[x\\,]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}({y}^{3}+1)^{1/2}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{2}{3}({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3/2}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/id #uuid "3477b5ca-309c-5b2a-b3fd-41dfc9bbf4db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7195" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/47f6b583b2dd059ad8df49b0ca4374a964b4a28e91eb242a0dd3c7b8476fd26e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 2x \\leq y \\leq 3-x \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/35b4b57f26c7903189899ec284faa39bb70fb3a80397b772376358d4d86b3ec1.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=3-y.\n$$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 2 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{3-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/id #uuid "eab89095-f5e5-53dc-9141-0f1a876f8a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-7196" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/47b8a129674d4644593741607e0efd5dea1ffde45357f327e3af4e08d0bf423d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq1, \\quad y^3 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = y^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\sqrt[3]{x},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny = 0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{x} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e627a8750d3f5055b3936d47100fc51ae68926dfdc0c53d020638c9ef99f9b27.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14626bd-0185-544a-bd6d-038a30e1fdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} {:answer/id #uuid "487185f9-2952-5dc0-a3b6-a9fc03088b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369475} {:answer/id #uuid "3798647d-bb41-500b-b07e-815b27c09528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{y-1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369476} {:answer/id #uuid "e47fe0e5-72bb-5853-a857-9f84ec5c4eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y+1}}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369477} {:answer/id #uuid "de26b309-5548-5387-94c8-7be223ba8459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y+1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14626bd-0185-544a-bd6d-038a30e1fdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b23d66-ae49-511e-815d-97cf66e9d038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cda48084-fd07-57a7-b084-a3c04ecffe6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132522" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/a65b8ea83edc85a0b0ec3f3df6ee6f5bc841e9cb5d634aab963054f836965146.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y-1},\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 5,\\,\\sqrt{y-1}\\le x\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/d10ba1db82f9d0366edb446f8bef564030fea2068066c7f05f388f0c9daa3414.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7059e4cf-4c01-52c0-a453-881bfdc07a09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{e}^{4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369481} {:answer/id #uuid "2666ae93-4f82-5862-bd1c-3c603e9ae4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369482} {:answer/id #uuid "35f55857-36e0-532f-ba35-f1bf2c708d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369483} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c0bfe2-6119-5b2a-b5cb-e5585b8d646b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369484} {:answer/id #uuid "38a543f1-70f6-5bb0-acf1-77bfcf8eaeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a543f1-70f6-5bb0-acf1-77bfcf8eaeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369485} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0b7e1c-b2d0-5f2e-9cd5-8cfe3fdcda32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac4f939e-e716-5d27-b164-1d86e556f507" :question/math-academy-id "q-132526" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7e0a0b1860f316f7954e85b9d7f6567d9e0c05bf316cb8af3901037abe6fd17e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,{e}^{y}\\le x\\le 5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3c95dd90ad02ab708f5893e47e7d7d0c8a212c18aed1972fb1bba1362181c383.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb8c9f3-bc80-5bc8-8cb9-022230329197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369488} {:answer/id #uuid "94a80f61-9e27-5661-bfa3-9b26f3c62014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369489} {:answer/id #uuid "d271c61b-d3ae-533d-9e2d-646259c78db3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369490} {:answer/id #uuid "c13786b1-25f3-5ccb-9812-367b73dd7fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369491} {:answer/id #uuid "afe7dd9f-98a6-5968-9a9f-5ebc98d0653b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb8c9f3-bc80-5bc8-8cb9-022230329197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369488} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c6f28c5-7391-54d4-a6ff-be0a527dd55c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9a7843d-3020-5482-8aef-e872811d9165" :question/math-academy-id "q-132565" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\le x\\le 1}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\le x\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,⟹\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\,⟹\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56fe5226-e329-5779-bacc-720b5ac5e32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x-1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} {:answer/id #uuid "b410f11e-25ce-5b16-866c-7fad5ee09d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x-1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369496} {:answer/id #uuid "51ac80e4-5f94-5f62-9814-1007c5258cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497} {:answer/id #uuid "3110bf60-4d27-501d-9edb-301cd788571a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369498} {:answer/id #uuid "73da39fa-9a16-5a18-bcf5-0f9b79b3589b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51ac80e4-5f94-5f62-9814-1007c5258cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b5437cd-07ed-5eda-b442-766b81b60c6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "446a02c7-d043-54a5-9d35-72c77f70ee6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-132573" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-1}^{1-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e6b8889f9e8025cfca1ee0ae61b67a2f7ec9ec4aee15fab78d2f418a655bbcb8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}-1\\le x\\le 1-y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/52640b30ded8bf2cd19fe7860c7640bde3c8427ae8978f32fb8020b2f7215468.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=1-x.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-1}^{1-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x+1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b47b8823-16b3-5969-8a1b-8f206f5dc0ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369502} {:answer/id #uuid "77ea91a6-9ad7-5ca5-b456-6f125376b6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369503} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf7ef9b-3b72-52cb-ab14-e2139cec8df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369504} {:answer/id #uuid "25767e96-4838-56b8-ac30-6e22cca5b6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369505} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0e42c4-7895-50b9-9667-bdf30f0318c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-2\\sqrt{x}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77ea91a6-9ad7-5ca5-b456-6f125376b6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369503} :answer-field/id #uuid "2476e7fd-dd6b-522b-becf-475e3c807e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1f0ad00-d863-57da-bab7-90e0c6534f2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132577" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}/4}^{{y}^{2}/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7212dd3ebd7ef05423c29ca6c3bd8524f8b336563d4618a960a375121c2b2157.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3eedf80b599bfaa97ad5cb42178359e24048938637f60a93e7ebb15cd8d8d2e4.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$  are \n\n$\\,$ $y=2\\sqrt{-x}\\,$ and $\\,y=2.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=2\\sqrt{x}\\,$ and $\\,y=2.$ \n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,2\\sqrt{-x}\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2\\sqrt{x}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{y}^{2}/4}^{{y}^{2}/4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{-x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9b5f5ef-9b99-5f5b-b0cc-9ac257ae7a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369509} {:answer/id #uuid "eb65f06d-de1e-5a3d-91d5-b1fb93976ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369510} {:answer/id #uuid "9a029056-dc77-50d6-aeee-01696dea412a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511} {:answer/id #uuid "d24382c1-10e8-58d0-b2da-a0025bcd1e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}-{e}^{-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369512} {:answer/id #uuid "43dcfdbb-7e7c-5918-88ce-9414ed51e49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8}-{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a029056-dc77-50d6-aeee-01696dea412a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e985df-366f-5045-8110-abecdf985e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce12cfe8-66ac-51ba-9613-47633a967b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-132638" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/262e1b0c4e0ddf338c527b487fcadc85baac12712f91cb4c54b771e49add25b9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/48c874b069b55599f76a8fe1aa50c5f4db36ff8943d264a107452668d689174b.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}4x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2(4-y){e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{8y-{y}^{2}}\\,{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={e}^{8(4)-{4}^{2}}-{e}^{8(0)-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f021f72e-8589-5435-931f-dc6a55b09b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2d9100-e583-5f3f-999a-e4d9f3f16c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369517} {:answer/id #uuid "45ff1de4-c070-546e-ac8d-0ab40a47f1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369518} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b0df37-666c-5efa-ac78-c097ed10555a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369519} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1cafa6-5400-5fa8-98b4-e1130e061d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b0df37-666c-5efa-ac78-c097ed10555a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369519} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f2577b-69b4-5ef6-a518-a4e625baa972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5e68c2e-86ca-55d4-99eb-62c0c258b9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132640" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x+2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3d51eade27c19494ac30ffdaf5baf8d30ffc9d18dcc3e1186cbc59412fe000c7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le x+2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x+2\\,⟹\\,x=y-2,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-2\\le x\\le 0},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/39072bd4c2d4bd763186f4d86921eaacb990df1e862f1c60b1f764ccff422e98.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{x+2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y-2}^{0}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}[{\\int }_{y-2}^{0}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\cdot x\\,{|}_{y-2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2-y){e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-{y}^{2}/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(2)-{2}^{2}/2}-{e}^{2(0)-{0}^{2}/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e235feb5-876d-5c86-bcf0-8faee9368476" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-35}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369523} {:answer/id #uuid "0a72cec3-e844-5738-992b-e2a2f49ba23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-15}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369524} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6babd1-6e92-5c39-85b7-96d77da4e4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-37}-{e}^{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9ab626-898f-56a2-b0b9-9cfe6edba241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-61}-{e}^{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526} {:answer/id #uuid "46e98f36-08ef-5edb-ae54-8a4b34278224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-45}-{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a9ab626-898f-56a2-b0b9-9cfe6edba241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-61}-{e}^{-7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526} :answer-field/id #uuid "4143003a-dad2-57e4-8248-f5ae2326c58c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c73d471-5c90-57d0-8932-12399b90a997" :question/math-academy-id "q-132644" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-x-3}^{1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/bb17a906727b436562060f84cb1acae42944587cdec6311627e30163485a72d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le -1,\\,-x-3\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-x-3\\,⟹\\,x=-y-3,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 1,\\,-y-3\\le x\\le -1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/f80d088bb764b5dd6aa1c6d26ebd7a5b2a3894c072d81966dec4975fc0e08e59.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-x-3}^{1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-y-3}^{-1}6x{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}[{\\int }_{-y-3}^{-1}6x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-y-3}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}3((-1)^{2}-(-y-3)^{2}){e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}3(1-(y+3)^{2}){e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{3y-(y+3)^{3}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{3(1)-(1+3)^{3}}-{e}^{3(-2)-(-2+3)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-61}-{e}^{-7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e65d4d89-6d9f-5938-bd12-c05375041c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{y}^{4 - y}∫_{0}^{4}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127473} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea747c-b1e6-500d-9358-f8d54bfa636b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127474} {:answer/id #uuid "45d72daf-a2a8-53fe-8d22-e55ff2a889c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{4 - y}^{y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127475} {:answer/id #uuid "76b11e55-10f0-5cd6-95d2-6ede8ffec86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127476} {:answer/id #uuid "7744d5a0-a627-5305-a4aa-8d3b166e4daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{4 - y}^{y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b11e55-10f0-5cd6-95d2-6ede8ffec86a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{2}∫_{y}^{4 - y}f(x, y)dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127476} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec08bfd6-54f4-5bc4-b805-d9be13c4db0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51957ec1-4df6-5139-b864-fabbb3776e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-38601" :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cfbb98-8a62-583e-aac7-2b12a4c0d097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{(x+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369530} {:answer/id #uuid "9d5daae2-c25f-58c7-9258-fbae5d5a9bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{(x+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} {:answer/id #uuid "69ac41a8-11fb-5634-878b-f896b2a6da6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(x+2)/2}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369532} {:answer/id #uuid "7711e55e-8439-5154-b784-b2f69bb4920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{(x+2)/2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369533} {:answer/id #uuid "1ceed4c4-a950-5cb8-a012-f6799f0d415c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(x+2)/2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d5daae2-c25f-58c7-9258-fbae5d5a9bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{(x+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} :answer-field/id #uuid "be012fa9-3b21-54b3-ba02-8da42a5841dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55e02e74-398d-5605-8097-c13c491d8f3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-82196" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2y-2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/062d32beb0cab13297c21b2eab703fd5e598191c96daa93a9c27337af95a1930.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2y-2\\le x\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=2y-2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{x+2}{2},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{x+2}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/543ffc57d0d32ae9a4bdefa87bd56deb7380061004b7737cf821117b4fb20e2f.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2y-2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{(x+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028b7f0c-1b93-5b68-a445-916457490121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369537} {:answer/id #uuid "6532fa84-8b74-503e-8e12-f4da035cd364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}{\\int }_{1}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369538} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7d1893-98ea-5abf-8480-f8664d87ce3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369539} {:answer/id #uuid "4eab79d8-7000-5c52-9d6b-9891a543d718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369540} {:answer/id #uuid "c23fdaa3-6cf6-542f-bb3e-e1cf589b25b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{e}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7d1893-98ea-5abf-8480-f8664d87ce3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369539} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa40179-f0e4-5cec-9b89-0b6a3553b5e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b8a5bf4-8a77-50ad-9ce7-60c229ebc078" :question/math-academy-id "q-82200" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/def6c95bc51de3e02b913d9951cce283285cd8049375c6842719c46d33a29a15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=e.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{e}^{y}\\le x\\le e},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e38a26dbfaa0c126ec31b44f2a7d31225666779bea7b6f8b67dc2cd04e703114.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbe043c8-5217-5f2d-97cf-7baf52beada7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369544} {:answer/id #uuid "3e9afb33-2e0e-57de-a994-b037b171c51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369545} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed62bd8-672a-5a85-bdb0-9d6e23412452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369546} {:answer/id #uuid "28837548-87e1-5023-93ba-57f829c13e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369547} {:answer/id #uuid "35e43536-8d9c-5695-b7e7-28409cd52c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e43536-8d9c-5695-b7e7-28409cd52c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369548} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28644c0-5a35-5bf7-88cd-028f0fe47658" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff29e666-6ba9-590a-b34c-62233d2ec1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82210" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/8287ba53b227ddc36aff486ea8d42a83203940ee265163320c19ad6cdb39089e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/2c9a41f06d7edfbad9756923d9b70f1d0ffd730350d0b7d2d0e109c1bca9f80d.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f52a0604-77f9-51c5-948a-710c52c26fe7" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df0e8245-6c32-5bba-9846-129f9f5c194f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{y+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369551} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5f1611-9bda-550d-a920-36ca1c76de60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{y+1}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369552} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74d9f7-82bf-5927-9811-db8aadd2a445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369553} {:answer/id #uuid "d049d4f5-44b7-5781-b324-bebd8253ecfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y+1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369554} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab39429-2fea-5d74-8590-1c1f77b683ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab39429-2fea-5d74-8590-1c1f77b683ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369555} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bcfb045-41d4-53a5-ba87-08d8e8a2d458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "086c680b-a52d-5b8f-9ea4-edf91606e5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-82255" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{\\sqrt{1-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/0e338b779e846bc3f5ac981c93f9cda65b63d654c1152aa423f855d12d7978d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-x}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/d2d1df8652af81fbcb789ac05a2d1bf43f9b5b12f394159d0eb17b4ecec90042.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$\\,$ $x=y+1.$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$\\,$ $x=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,0\\le x\\le y+1}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{\\sqrt{1-x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ecfd970-b19a-5331-84dc-2e268793a3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369558} {:answer/id #uuid "44e39233-4098-5eba-8824-ca8a3c2171cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369559} {:answer/id #uuid "c7283105-fc3f-5bd1-895d-4c59ada2283e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369560} {:answer/id #uuid "86c3582d-b36c-5f7a-9786-9fd774615db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369561} {:answer/id #uuid "5eaac8ee-962d-5a0b-9e1d-8446c2d384cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ecfd970-b19a-5331-84dc-2e268793a3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369558} :answer-field/id #uuid "71092328-12d0-55e5-ac6d-8f789fee8802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c655b65b-a874-5364-940d-5589bdee1ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-82292" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/fb111bd5365ad339dcddbf5e3d98b5e53cfc029ed88c3785154194f1e64d3297.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/0bed4692a7a6851ef2b66cb3842ebac6b2ef7ef4ff30bed41e84b7cb71c4f5c0.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})}{4}[{x}^{4}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{3})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1723a1-c676-517c-8dc5-f2b61ba017c6" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Swapping the Order of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83abcf17-e462-5dda-bc1f-b0561e158bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{8}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369565} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e867dd-0f5a-53a2-ab6a-ea67452ec103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{16}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369566} {:answer/id #uuid "0dad88f7-d138-5b9a-907a-30508a5c76c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369567} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2a3bf0-1628-5889-bb54-cd948ef3768b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369568} {:answer/id #uuid "84dcd39d-c93b-5a82-b8e2-ac62d4a18195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-{e}^{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f2a3bf0-1628-5889-bb54-cd948ef3768b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369568} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ffcee8-7d7e-5b2c-a00c-997a5d79b78f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcb4996c-00f3-5286-ad6a-3370249bd19d" :question/math-academy-id "q-82296" :question/problem "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/d5d777577f85cd577ce35bfd3a99c1e2218622d1856f4fe7ee4afe1cfd2c9f86.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 8,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}\\le x\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. Its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/05ec37a8f4fd33c05a2438df5c5fbc697f8b560fde0b9fe73922a0db56a09c2c.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{4}}[y]_{0}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,{e}^{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{{x}^{4}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{16}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
